  "interpretation": "Existing SG2RotationMatrixWords schema defines nine signed WORDs in row-major offsets0,2,4/6,8,10/12,14,16. Existing app matrix instances atF4/106 already use it; original source scratch2BFC..2C0E and stored player orientation2C0E..2C20 were only data seeds. Native LEAs, exact18-byte copies (four LONGs plus one WORD), signed row dot-products and the builder/transposer establish that same record structure for these two source-backed mutable buffers. Create canonical source owners then bind the existing type through normal element commands. No runtime-only numeric-overlap alias, guessed pointer scan or per-instruction register seed. Original2B1E calls the three-angle builder then swaps WORD offsets2/6,4/12,10/14 using D0 and saves/restores D0/A5: literal in-place transpose. It is not a proof quantized matrices form an exact inverse or every input is orthogonal. Original29BA reads three model rotations, handles its zero-angle/non-type30 shortcut and otherwise combines original built/view matrix values; its output feeds model_view_matrix. Preserve all sign choices, fixed-point ASL2/SWAP truncation and overflow, original caller assumptions and type30 branch. Source buffers remain mutable original image data; no changed initialization, relocation or all-caller/lifetime guarantee. Nearby duplicate WORD11E initializations remain unnamed: inspected accepted paths provide no demonstrated cache reader, and absent native direct readers do not prove dead storage or an original bug.",
  "cause": "App-resident matrices already used SG2RotationMatrixWords. Two original source-backed mutable buffers had only raw data seeds, with no canonical typed source owner; LEA-based consumers therefore lacked their field schema.",
  "changes": "Create two canonical18-byte owners and bind the existing custom type through normal element commands. Existing native source-target/type propagation then recovers all51 sites here. No new framework code, per-instruction register seeds, numeric-overlap runtime substitutes or generated-source edits.",
